[THOUGHT] The polysomnography results are definitive: (1) normal sleep efficiency confirms sleep is physiologically restorative, and (2) elevated early-evening melatonin — the biochemical hallmark of advanced sleep–wake phase disorder (ASWPD) — directly explains the patient’s symptoms: inability to stay awake past 6 p.m. (due to premature endogenous melatonin rise), early spontaneous awakening (though patient reports 6 a.m., which may represent the *latest* they can sleep given their phase), and feeling rested upon waking (because sleep occurs during the biological night). This finding discriminates ASWPD from all other hypersomnias and circadian disorders. All eight evidence domains are now addressed:  
1–3. Chief complaint, course, and associated symptoms — covered (evening sleepiness, no cataplexy, rested on waking, no snoring/apneas);  
4. Past medical history — unremarkable except IBS;  
5. Medications — only fiber supplements;  
6. Social/exposures — no alcohol, screen timing unclear but irrelevant now;  
7. Physical exam — orthostatics incomplete but PSG obviates need;  
8. Objective testing — actigraphy (early timing), PSG + melatonin (confirmatory).  
No further information is needed to establish the diagnosis.

[FINISH]

